{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"recursion","title":"Recursion","group":"Functions","order":18,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A recursive function calls itself on a smaller input until it reaches a base case. Every call gets its own stack frame.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Anatomy</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nlong long factorial(int n) {\n    if (n &lt;= 1) return 1;              // base case\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);       // recursive case: smaller problem\n}\n\nint power(int b, int e) {              // fast exponentiation: O(log e)\n    if (e == 0) return 1;\n    int half = power(b, e / 2);\n    return e % 2 ? half * half * b : half * half;\n}\n\nbool isPalindrome(const std::string&amp; s, int l, int r) {\n    if (l &gt;= r) return true;\n    return s[l] == s[r] &amp;&amp; isPalindrome(s, l + 1, r - 1);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; factorial(20) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; power(3, 13) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; isPalindrome(\"racecar\", 0, 6) &lt;&lt; isPalindrome(\"rocket\", 0, 5) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2432902008176640000\n1594323\n10</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i>Backtracking: generate all subsets</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nvoid subsets(const std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; nums, std::size_t i, std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; cur) {\n    if (i == nums.size()) {\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; '{';\n        for (int x : cur) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"} \";\n        return;\n    }\n    subsets(nums, i + 1, cur);          // exclude nums[i]\n    cur.push_back(nums[i]);             // include nums[i]\n    subsets(nums, i + 1, cur);\n    cur.pop_back();                     // undo (backtrack)\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; cur;\n    subsets({1, 2, 3}, 0, cur);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{} {3} {2} {23} {1} {13} {12} {123}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Memoisation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nlong long fib(int n, std::vector&lt;long long&gt;&amp; memo) {\n    if (n &lt; 2) return n;\n    if (memo[n] != -1) return memo[n];          // already computed\n    return memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;long long&gt; memo(91, -1);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; fib(90, memo) &lt;&lt; '\\n';          // instant; naive recursion would take ages\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2880067194370816120</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Deep recursion overflows the stack (default ~1–8 MB): a recursive DFS on a 10⁶-node path graph will crash. Convert to an explicit stack/loop when depth can be large.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Recursion</th><th>Iteration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Readability</td><td>Natural for trees, divide &amp; conquer, backtracking</td><td>Natural for linear scans</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>O(depth) stack frames</td><td>O(1) extra (or an explicit stack)</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>Stack overflow</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does C++ guarantee tail-call optimisation?</summary><div><p>No. Compilers often turn tail calls into jumps at <code>-O2</code>, but the standard doesn't require it and destructors of locals can prevent it. Don't rely on it for unbounded recursion depth.</p></div></details></section>"}